WEBVTT

00:00:00.573 --> 00:00:26.133
<v Riley>Hey there, good to have you. Today on Arts, Culture and Digital Storytelling: Lagos designers turning busy city streets into runways, with a fashion house reportedly calling. We've got Meta's settlement of up to $16.7 billion over teen safety, and Bowie's 1983 jab at MTV for never playing Black artists. Then the deep dive: when a ritual widens its circle, who actually holds the pen? Stick around.

00:00:39.608 --> 00:00:59.200
<v Riley>Welcome to Cariboo Signals, an AI-generated review of the latest tech news and ideas that impact our rural communities. I'm Riley. It's Monday, November 9th, 2026. Today's theme is Arts, Culture &amp; Digital Storytelling. Reaching you from the heart of the Cariboo, the homeland of the Secwépemc, Tŝilhqot'in, and Dakelh nations.

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:22.768
<v Casey>And I'm Casey. We're looking at who makes the stories, and who controls the channels they travel on. Out at Horsefly Lake this morning it's mainly clear and around minus one, heading for a high of 9 with moderate drizzle through the day. Across the Cariboo, 100 Mile House is minus one, Williams Lake is at zero under clear skies, Quesnel is minus one and clear, and Anahim Lake sits at minus four.

00:01:22.968 --> 00:01:24.408
<v Riley>Starting with the news.

00:01:25.808 --> 00:01:59.398
<v Riley>We start at home with the mill and the power lines, then turn to a run of stories about who tells stories and who owns the channels they travel on. Millar Western's curtailment at Quesnel River Pulp was first set to run from September 4th to November 1st. My Cariboo Now's update, along with Treefrog Creative's account of the same story, says the shutdown now continues to December 1st. The company blames sustained weak demand, poor pricing, reduced order volumes, and significant tariffs on Chinese exports. Employees have been promised an update by mid-November.

00:01:59.848 --> 00:02:04.920
<v Casey>So the update lands halfway through, and the mill stays quiet until December 1st either way.

00:02:06.343 --> 00:02:20.079
<v Riley>The same page reports that Canada's 2026 federal budget proposes $6.5 billion for forest sector transformation. That money comes through a federal budget line, and the mill reopens on its own timetable.

00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:53.322
<v Casey>BC Hydro says about 63,000 customers were still without power on Sunday morning. At the storm's peak, around 1 a.m., the figure was about 135,000. More than 380,000 outages have been reported since Friday. Spokesperson Whitney Deane said crews were ready, but winds were expected to cause more damage before conditions improved. Prince George, Surrey, Duncan, and Port Hardy were among the hardest hit. Environment Canada put gusts at 60 to 80 kilometres an hour across the Interior, peaking Sunday morning.

00:02:53.771 --> 00:02:58.075
<v Riley>A storm that peaks at one in the morning is one people wake up to in the dark.

00:02:59.750 --> 00:03:33.988
<v Riley>In India, the Digital News Publishers Association, which represents 21 media organizations, has asked the Supreme Court to review the Digital Personal Data Protection Act. The law passed in 2023, and the government notified its phased rules in November 2025. The association argues that the law has no exemption for public-interest journalism. It says consent requirements and data-erasure rights could complicate reporting and digital archives, and that the government's information-gathering powers raise concerns about protecting sources.

00:03:34.438 --> 00:03:37.470
<v Casey>The archive is the part a small paper can least afford to lose.

00:03:39.251 --> 00:04:03.715
<v Casey>The Tyee asks how local journalism can be saved. It says B.C. newsrooms have been gutted, and the reporting teams that remain are trying to do more with less. Its argument is that corporate consolidation hasn't worked, and it suggests a return to small independent, mom-and-pop outlets as a possible future. The piece also notes that local politics shape daily life, yet local elections often draw the lowest turnout of any kind of vote.

00:04:04.165 --> 00:04:09.357
<v Riley>Lowest turnout and thinnest coverage is a bad pairing for anyone holding a council to account.

00:04:11.053 --> 00:04:41.717
<v Riley>Open Culture revisits David Bowie's 1983 complaint to MTV about the network's failure to play music videos by Black artists. The post traces the phrase "Will it play in Peoria?" to the late nineteenth century and Horatio Alger's novel Five Hundred Dollars. It argues that broadcasters and writers still program for a presumed audience, which the piece describes as insular, culturally conservative, unfailingly white, and fearful of anyone who isn't. That assumption has outlived the evidence.

00:04:42.167 --> 00:04:46.927
<v Casey>Peoria stands in for small towns, and the guess about who lives there was wrong all along.

00:04:48.635 --> 00:05:28.465
<v Casey>Mark Lanier was lead attorney in the first social media addiction case to go to trial. In March it ended in a verdict against Meta and Google, with $6 million in damages. Speaking at the WIRED World Fair, Lanier called the award a symbolic win and a rounding error for the companies. The bigger number came from a separate case. Attorneys general from several states accused Meta of failing to protect young users. One of those states was Arkansas, where Lanier's firm served as outside counsel. A few days into that trial in August, Meta agreed to a settlement of up to $16.7 billion, to be paid out to 51 US states and territories. It also pledged changes to how teenagers use Instagram.

00:05:28.915 --> 00:05:34.507
<v Riley>Six million on one side and sixteen billion on the other. The state cases are what move a platform.

00:05:36.305 --> 00:05:55.473
<v Riley>Ofcom, the UK's communications regulator, has set out how it wants social media platforms to keep under-16s off their services. The ban is expected to start at some point next year. A ban that exists on paper still needs a way to check who is old enough, and the regulator's age-check plan is what will decide whether this one works.

00:05:56.928 --> 00:06:25.734
<v Casey>In Lagos, creative Rachel Ojuromi has spent six months turning busy city streets into a runway. With her stylist friend Debby Fasingha, she makes videos of designs built from thrifted clothes and scraps. Most of the material comes from open-air hubs such as Yaba market. They film in Gbagada, Yaba, and other parts of Lagos without disrupting traffic, and pedestrians, traders and drivers answer with stares, smiles and cheers. The Guardian reports that a fashion house came calling, and the headline names Chanel and Paris.

00:06:26.184 --> 00:06:31.032
<v Riley>The market was the wardrobe and the street was the set. They never needed a studio.

00:06:32.637 --> 00:07:07.699
<v Riley>Hyperallergic's election guide for artists and arts workers set out what was at stake in the US midterms: arts funding, censorship, AI regulation, and affordability. Its reasoning is that Congress decides appropriations for federal arts bodies such as the National Endowment for the Arts, and Republicans hold narrow majorities in both chambers. The guide profiles candidates who are artists themselves, among them Claire Valdez in New York and musician Bobby Pulido in Texas. The midterms were held on November 3rd, so the vote the guide was written for has now taken place.

00:07:08.148 --> 00:07:12.132
<v Casey>The dull budget line decides whether anyone gets paid, and the guide knows it.

00:07:13.756 --> 00:07:28.164
<v Casey>Podcasthon 2027 is open for free registration. Any podcaster who can publish an episode by March 19th, 2027, can sign up. A small team and a group of volunteers run the event, and they are looking for sponsors to help secure its future.

00:07:29.671 --> 00:07:39.831
<v Riley>The organizers followed up with a second message, titled "Everything you need to know, as promised!" It went out through a newsletter platform and repeats the sign-up call.

00:07:40.281 --> 00:07:42.809
<v Casey>Same organizers, and the same ask for sponsors.

00:07:44.535 --> 00:07:55.207
<v Casey>The Atlantic reports that people who work in children's books see too few male protagonists in today's titles. Several told the magazine that the shortage makes it harder to get boys of that age reading.

00:07:55.657 --> 00:07:59.273
<v Riley>Protagonists are a publishing decision, and a few offices can change them.

00:08:01.049 --> 00:08:21.409
<v Riley>The New York Times traces the long history of triumphal arches and asks where the proposed United States Triumphal Arch fits in. The design would rise to 250 feet, which would make it taller than the Mexican arch and the tallest in the world. It is going ahead despite opposition from architectural and preservation organizations.

00:08:22.989 --> 00:08:37.853
<v Casey>An Akwesasne family is carrying on a 16th-century Haudenosaunee tradition. For the first time, 10-year-old Kenna is back-weaving the threads of a beaded loom belt. APTN's story is about the handover, a young weaver taking up the work inside her family.

00:08:38.303 --> 00:08:41.927
<v Riley>A first time at ten. Nobody learns that from a press release.

00:08:43.721 --> 00:09:23.439
<v Riley>Resilience.org tells how Seattle neighbourhoods turned improvised gatherings into emergency hubs. The 2006 windstorm hit on Hanukkah Eve, with gusts of 100 miles an hour on the coast and 80 inland. More than 1.8 million residences and businesses lost power across the region. Eighteen people died, most of them from carbon monoxide poisoning in the days that followed, after running generators in enclosed spaces. In West Seattle, residents met each morning in parks and on street corners to share information and help one another. After the power came back, several neighbourhoods made those meeting points permanent.

00:09:23.889 --> 00:09:27.817
<v Casey>Somebody organized those street corners before the city ever called them hubs.

00:09:29.477 --> 00:09:47.445
<v Casey>Resilience.org has republished an essay by Gracy Olmstead, first run in her Granola newsletter, that argues for craft and thrift over conspicuous consumption. It draws on Haley Stewart's book The Grace of Enough. Its model is the Weasleys from Harry Potter, a poor, loud, messy family with an empty bank vault and plenty of joy.

00:09:47.895 --> 00:09:51.039
<v Riley>Thrift makes a good dinner table, but it won't keep a mill running.

00:09:52.816 --> 00:10:12.016
<v Riley>Atlas Obscura lists the Tomba dei Giganti S'Ena e Thimes in Dorgali, Italy, a Bronze Age tomb about five minutes' walk from the SP 38 road. The directional arrows are stones laid on the ground, and each one is unique. The central stone of the tomb's front face has an opening at its base that you can look through.

00:10:13.437 --> 00:10:34.005
<v Casey>Renovate Europe Day took place on November 5th, during Ireland's EU presidency, at the Department of Climate's offices in Dublin. Renovate Europe and the Irish Green Building Council organized it with the Sustainable Energy Authority of Ireland. The event asked how Europe can renovate its building stock at scale while keeping projects affordable, accessible and high quality.

00:10:34.455 --> 00:10:38.823
<v Riley>Retrofits at scale are a cost question before they're a design question.

00:10:39.273 --> 00:10:44.585
<v Casey>That's the roundup. Closer to home now, we turn to a group that keeps live culture running in Williams Lake.

00:10:45.985 --> 00:11:12.497
<v Riley>The Central Cariboo Arts &amp; Culture Society keeps a lot of Williams Lake's creative life going. It manages the Arts Centre, runs the grants programs, and puts on Performances in the Park, the summer concert series where people bring a lawn chair and the kids dance near the front. If you have an arts project that needs a boost, or you just want to know what's on, the society is a good first call. You'll find them at centralcaribooarts.com.

00:11:13.122 --> 00:11:21.298
<v Casey>Performances in the Park pays its artists and sound technicians, and its organizers are paid staff. I'd like every arts group to copy that part.

00:11:21.398 --> 00:11:26.182
<v Casey>Next, Remembrance Day, and the question of who gets to shape a story a town tells every year.

00:11:27.582 --> 00:12:12.748
<v Riley>Remembrance Day began as Armistice Day, marking the end of the First World War. The 100 Mile Free Press piece says it has since grown to recognize veterans of the Second World War, the Boer War, the Korean War, and the Afghanistan Mission, along with UN peacekeepers and members of the RCMP. A ritual that has taken on that much is cultural infrastructure. It's a plain format, a service and a parade, and it lets many groups and households join in and widen the story as they go. So the question for this morning starts there. When the people who have carried a ritual for years get the places in it, are we rewarding the ones who did the work, or are we keeping out the person who just arrived with something new to add?

00:12:13.048 --> 00:12:20.752
<v Casey>Start with the list. Every addition on it was a decision. A story can widen through a string of quiet choices made by whoever holds the pen.

00:12:21.034 --> 00:12:25.134
<v Riley>Someone always holds the pen. What matters is whether it can change hands.

00:12:25.134 --> 00:12:26.070
<v Casey>So who holds it here?

00:12:26.399 --> 00:12:45.119
<v Riley>In 100 Mile House it's Royal Canadian Legion Branch 260. It organizes the largest service in the region, on Wednesday, November 11th. The parade goes down Birch Avenue at 10:30 a.m., and marchers assemble in the 100 Mile Elementary School parking lot at 10:15.

00:12:45.729 --> 00:12:55.873
<v Casey>Look at the order of that list. The Boer War came before the First World War, yet it's listed after the Second. The names weren't added in sequence. Somebody chose to add each one.

00:12:56.460 --> 00:13:03.052
<v Riley>The order cuts both ways, though. A committee that adds a war from 1899 went looking for someone it had missed.

00:13:03.676 --> 00:13:10.228
<v Casey>Or it filled a slot. From the curb you can't tell which, and that's the difference between a memorial and a mailing list.

00:13:10.905 --> 00:13:17.721
<v Riley>The list still shows the ritual responding to the community. Somebody pushed for recognition, and the service made room.

00:13:18.261 --> 00:13:23.421
<v Casey>Somebody pushed. Who pushed, and who said yes, lives in minutes nobody outside the branch reads.

00:13:23.521 --> 00:13:32.449
<v Casey>The other South Cariboo communities hold their own services the same morning, organized by people the public notice leaves unnamed. That's where I'd start asking.

00:13:32.993 --> 00:13:41.497
<v Riley>One organizer is how almost any event gets built. The Legion sets the order and calls the groups. The parade itself proves it brings others in.

00:13:42.172 --> 00:13:52.796
<v Casey>And who keeps it running in year five? A volunteer committee that does the work once is a committee. One that does it for a decade is a system, and from outside you can't tell which one this is.

00:13:53.435 --> 00:14:01.451
<v Riley>It's a repeatable format, and that's the strength of it. Once the order of march exists, next year's parade costs less to put together.

00:14:01.738 --> 00:14:05.090
<v Casey>Less effort, maybe. The authority stays exactly where it was.

00:14:05.718 --> 00:14:17.750
<v Riley>Then take the strongest case for a Legion-run service. Running it is what a Legion is for. Veterans organize the veterans' day, and the branch has the hall, the flag, and the history. That's a feature.

00:14:18.299 --> 00:14:25.667
<v Casey>For members, sure. For somebody who moved to 100 Mile House last spring, it depends on whether anyone asked what the day should include.

00:14:26.263 --> 00:14:31.319
<v Riley>So ask. A branch that publishes its planning meetings can be asked, and that's a cheap test.

00:14:31.983 --> 00:14:36.447
<v Casey>Cheap for the branch. Expensive for anyone who doesn't already know the calendar.

00:14:37.122 --> 00:14:51.882
<v Riley>Then look at who's in the line. Legion members, the 2887 Rocky Mountain Rangers Royal Canadian Army Cadet Corps, 100 Mile House RCMP, the 100 Mile Girl Guides, and other organizations, all marching in the same parade.

00:14:52.563 --> 00:15:07.587
<v Casey>Every one of those is an established institution. The cadets have a corps, the Guides have a troop, the RCMP has a detachment. A group formed this year has no corps and no troop. Whether it could join the line, or help shape what gets said at the cenotaph, is the open part.

00:15:08.253 --> 00:15:19.893
<v Riley>Established groups keep turning up, and a parade rewards that. The cadets are also a pipeline. A corps is where young people learn the drill, and some of them will be running the parade in twenty years.

00:15:20.533 --> 00:15:29.157
<v Casey>That pipeline only works for kids who can get to the drill hall every week. In a region where the long drive is a standing cost, the pipeline has a postcode.

00:15:29.785 --> 00:15:36.521
<v Riley>Then it's a design problem, and design problems have fixes. A corps can run drill nights in more than one place.

00:15:36.804 --> 00:15:41.116
<v Casey>It can. Until somebody publishes where the drill nights are, that's a promise.

00:15:41.216 --> 00:15:48.424
<v Casey>So that's where fairness bites. The people already in the line get the spots, and the newcomer waits for a seat nobody has opened.

00:15:49.049 --> 00:15:58.401
<v Riley>Reliability earns the place. A group that shows up year after year has earned a spot in the line, and the next group can start the same way, by showing up.

00:15:59.038 --> 00:16:06.486
<v Casey>That's a rule about time served. It says nothing about who gets asked. A group that arrives this year has no ten-year record to point to.

00:16:06.821 --> 00:16:11.309
<v Riley>Then ask the branch how a new group gets in line. That question has an answer.

00:16:11.581 --> 00:16:15.605
<v Casey>And if the answer is "call us in March," that tells you who the calendar is for.

00:16:16.229 --> 00:16:20.989
<v Riley>It's still usable. Now the calendar is known, and the next group can plan around it.

00:16:21.589 --> 00:16:25.565
<v Casey>Mm-hm. And the newcomer still hears about March from someone on the inside.

00:16:26.154 --> 00:16:31.738
<v Riley>The roster is visible, though. A named list in a public parade means anybody watching can see who's marching.

00:16:32.034 --> 00:16:34.266
<v Casey>You can read a roster without knowing who wrote it.

00:16:34.864 --> 00:16:41.008
<v Riley>It tells you who's marching. The planning meetings cover the rest, and anyone can ask about those.

00:16:41.637 --> 00:16:49.637
<v Casey>They can. The public notice gives the time, the assembly point, and the route down Birch Avenue. How the program gets set is a separate question.

00:16:50.236 --> 00:17:01.084
<v Riley>And the households have a place. The piece describes the day as a time for everyone to come together for an hour to remember, and says the services give households a direct way to take part.

00:17:01.605 --> 00:17:09.053
<v Casey>That covers who can come. Who can speak is a different matter. Attending puts you in the audience, and the program is decided elsewhere.

00:17:09.659 --> 00:17:20.979
<v Riley>Attending is participation. An hour-long service is about as easy to join as anything in town. Standing on the curb is how a kid learns the words, and that's how the story survives.

00:17:21.489 --> 00:17:26.537
<v Casey>It survives in the form it already has. Joining is easy. Changing what it says is the hard part.

00:17:27.047 --> 00:17:35.495
<v Riley>So attendance is the open door, and authorship is the question. Even so, authorship has to exist somewhere before anyone can walk through that door.

00:17:36.125 --> 00:17:45.301
<v Casey>Then ask for what would make it exist. A printed program. A list of who sits on the planning group. A note on what was added to the service, when, and at whose request.

00:17:45.893 --> 00:17:57.301
<v Riley>And ask for next year's order of march before it's printed. If the branch decides alone, the community can plan around that. If it doesn't, the committee members should be named somewhere public.

00:17:57.878 --> 00:18:02.590
<v Casey>The record is the test. Right now it probably lives with whoever is on this year's committee.

00:18:03.247 --> 00:18:12.415
<v Riley>So the cheap fix is a shared program file that the branch and the marching groups all hold. Any group can read it, and any group can propose a change.

00:18:13.104 --> 00:18:22.120
<v Casey>That helps the groups already marching. It won't stop a year-five committee from deciding alone unless the file has a keeper with a term and a way to be replaced.

00:18:22.792 --> 00:18:28.576
<v Riley>Then the structural question is who keeps the file, and whether groups outside the Legion get a turn in that role.

00:18:28.910 --> 00:18:31.526
<v Casey>Maybe. I'd want to see the file before I believed it.

00:18:32.064 --> 00:18:40.128
<v Riley>Then test it. Pull the program from two years back and lay it beside this year's. Any change in the order means somebody made a decision.

00:18:40.688 --> 00:18:55.448
<v Casey>Now take the same question across town to the stage. The Central Cariboo Arts &amp; Culture Society runs the Arts Centre, the grants, and Performances in the Park. It pays its artists and sound technicians, and its organizers are paid staff, so that work has a budget line.

00:18:55.769 --> 00:18:59.905
<v Riley>Good. A paid crew means a budget line that someone has to defend every year.

00:19:00.549 --> 00:19:09.877
<v Casey>A budget line doesn't tell you who gets the stage. Does the summer series go to the acts that have played every season, or to the act that just showed up with new songs?

00:19:10.163 --> 00:19:30.667
<v Riley>On that question the stage has an answer the parade doesn't. The series has no grant panel handing out slots. It's a curated concert series, produced by the Community Arts Council of Williams Lake, and it features artists from across British Columbia. A curator drawing from the whole province isn't simply rewarding whoever volunteered longest.

00:19:31.336 --> 00:19:40.128
<v Casey>A curator is still one pen. Curated from across B.C. could mean the touring act from Victoria gets the slot over the fiddler who moved to Williams Lake in June.

00:19:40.676 --> 00:19:49.908
<v Riley>Or it means there's a person whose whole job is to go looking, and you can write to that person. That's more of a door than a parade committee offers.

00:19:50.488 --> 00:19:57.952
<v Casey>It's a named door, which helps. I'd still want each season's lineup published next to the last one, the same request as the parade order.

00:19:58.603 --> 00:20:03.555
<v Riley>Program first, then the people who chose it. That holds for a parade and for a stage.

00:20:04.218 --> 00:20:18.098
<v Casey>Then there are the costs nobody budgets. The route, the traffic arrangements, the volunteer hours that put a service on Birch Avenue at 10:30. None of that shows up in a notice, and it falls on whoever is still volunteering next November.

00:20:18.787 --> 00:20:31.027
<v Riley>That's the answer to the fairness worry, too. If those costs are listed publicly, a newcomer can see what it takes to help carry them, and helping carry them is how you earn a spot without a ten-year record.

00:20:31.622 --> 00:20:38.990
<v Casey>So who should be able to see how the scope and the program get decided? The branch, the groups marching, or the households on the curb?

00:20:39.318 --> 00:20:43.014
<v Riley>And what's lost if each one assumes another is keeping the record?

00:20:43.358 --> 00:20:49.902
<v Casey>Whatever the next volunteer needed to know. Nobody goes back to look for it, and the newcomer never finds out there was a door.

00:20:50.451 --> 00:21:05.595
<v Riley>I land on the cheap tools. A shared program file, a published lineup, and a named curator would let the people who've done the work keep their place while a newcomer can see the way in. Whether the people who decide will open those records is still unproven.

00:21:06.253 --> 00:21:24.197
<v Casey>Unproven is the right word. Until somebody lays two years of programs side by side, the reward for helping and the lockout of the newcomer look identical from the curb. If you march on Wednesday, organize a stage, or watched and wished you had a say, tell us how your group decides at feedback at cariboo signals dot c-a.

00:21:24.860 --> 00:21:32.356
<v Riley>And if you're marching, the line forms in the elementary school parking lot at 10:15. Have a good Monday, everyone.

00:21:32.956 --> 00:21:52.108
